diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation found no active fault. Every diagnostic layer — L2 identity, addressing, OSPF control plane, traffic control, and resource pressure — returned clean.

Phase 1 reachability confirmed 0% loss across all sampled inter-campus paths including the specifically reported pc1 (10.5.0.1) → 172.16.30.14 pair. Direct shell probes from pc1 to all three reported hosts (172.16.30.12, 172.16.30.13, 172.16.30.14) returned replies with 0% loss and ~2 ms RTT. The observed TTL of 58 (64 − 6 hops) matches the expected forwarding path exactly: pc1 → sshswitch → router17 → router7 (via router8 or router10) → router16 → mswitch0 → destination host.

Inspection of the FRR routing tables on router7, router17, and sshswitch confirmed that 172.16.30.0/24 carries identical OSPF cost and next-hop chain as 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24 — the two Campus 1 subnets the user claims work fine. There is no routing asymmetry between the three VLAN subnets. All nine OSPF routers have fully converged neighbours and 18 routes apiece. No ACL rules, no tc qdisc shaping, no MAC conflicts, no static ARP entries, and no resource pressure were found on any device.

Root cause: No fault is present in the live topology. The symptom reported in ticket #8090 cannot be reproduced from current state, and there is no residual mechanism (flap script, misconfigured rule, stale route) that would explain a past occurrence. The complaint is unconfirmed by live evidence.

Fix: No corrective action is required. If the user still experiences the problem, further investigation should focus on application-layer behaviour on the end-hosts (firewall on the PCs themselves, application binding to a specific interface, or a client-side routing policy) rather than the campus network fabric.
